    "turn_text": "Welcome to the Blyte Politics Podcast. Political issues are too important to be decided by grown adults behaving like five-year-olds on the playground. You're once a weak guide to the real issues facing America. We'll forget the bias and the yelling. They're good, honest, intelligent people on both sides of most political issues. And just tell you what's really happening in America. Welcome into the Play Politics Podcast. I'm your host Tyler Page. I've got a great show lined up for you today. On the show, Representative Rob Bishop, Congressman from the great state of Utah, is going to be our guest. He's going to break a little bit of news and talk a lot about life in Washington and what he's doing as head of the Natural Resource Committee. In addition, we're going to talk about free speech and light of those shootings down in Texas and how our media has gotten this completely wrong. You might even hear me raise my voice just a little bit. But starting off, this first segment is all presidential election coverage. At the center of our presidential election coverage nationally is, of course, Hillary Clinton, the presumptive Democratic nominee. Hillary this week made news by announcing that she would expand President Obama's executive amnesty program. Now, what was President Obama's amnesty? Congress is and has been at a stalemate on immigration for nearly a decade now. Republicans believe that our border needs to be enforced. more strictly. Democrats believe that we need to find a peaceful solution so that people who are here and who have been here peacefully without breaking the law for decades can have the right to get on a path to citizenship, can pay a fine, something like that. There are some Republicans across one way, there are some Democrats across the other way, but those are generally the feelings here. President Bush, during his second term, tried to push legislation to do both of those things together. It failed, and at that time Senator Obama fought against it. However, President Obama, since he's been president, has been in favor of immigration reform that looks pretty much exactly like President Bush's. Just after the 2014 election, he demanded Congress pass reform or he do something about it. He did. He gave an executive order, you may have heard about it, showed up on Saturday Night Live and other places, where you pretty much just said prosecutors were not going to chase people who are here illegally that meet certain criteria, essentially they hadn't committed crimes they've been here for a certain amount of time. even allowed them to sign up and get an ID card protecting them from prosecution. Now, this is currently held up in the courts. It's controversial as to whether or not it's legal. There is no question that the president has the authority to direct where we use our legal resources. So the reality is we don't have police officers or at the federal level FBI agents, ICE, agents and others to be able to enforce every little law and get every little infraction and that's generally a good thing. If you go a mile per hour over the speed limit, my guess is you probably don't want to get pulled over and you don't expect to get pulled over for going a mile per hour over the speed limit. There's discretion there. The question is whether President Obama was creating a broad policy or whether he was just using his prosecutorial discretion. And the courts are debating this right now. Well, it's on hold right now because of a lower court ruling. And it's being appealed up, but we'll see what happens. My guess, it ends at the Supreme Court. But regardless of what you think of this immigration policy, and regardless of what you think of what it does, Hillary has proposed that she would expand it as president to include more people. Speaking of the Clintons, let's talk a little bit about presidential coverage. Hillary has only answered seven media questions since she declared for public office, and the first sit-down interview, in-depth interview, given by a Clinton since she declared her run for office is from Bill, which is hilarious because her spokesman then comes out and says, well, this isn't about Bill, it's about Hillary. But Bill is the highest profile spokesperson they have, and they use him more than her. take it for what is worth at least so far. So Bill was on a jaunt to Africa. We talked about last week the Safari was some of the donors that came and was visiting with a reporter and the reporter asked about the Clinton Foundation and about his speeches. He said he's not going to stop giving speeches because he quote needs to pay the bills. Now that one was a really, really poor statement because of course he's made over $100 million in speeches. And so the notion that he needs to quote unquote pay the bills didn't go over well. It's kind of like Hillary a few months back said they were quote unquote dead broke when they left the White House. They did have some some legal obligations due to Bill's conduct and we'll get to that in a second But regardless for people who have hundreds of millions dollars more money than most Americans and their entire families will see in their lifetime This one that just really hurt and went really below the belt now look Bill said something here, but before he said it, Mark Halprin, who wrote the definitive book on the 2008 election called Game Change, made the comment that the Clintons are held to a higher standard than others. And I just have to react to that. That is ridiculous. And it leads to our first edition of our segment whenever somebody tells a major league lie.",
