    "turn_text": "Let's be clear here. In 1984, Gary Hart was on path to the Democratic nomination until he was having an extramarital affair. All of a sudden, his campaign was over. In the 1990s, when Clarence Thomas was up to be Supreme Court Justice in his hearings, Democrats brought in a woman who claimed he had sexually harassed her by making some dirty comments at her. A few years later, President Clinton is repeatedly accused of sexual harassment, He's actually accused of rape a couple times, open about the fact that he's had extramarital affairs, and it's well-known and well documented. And he gets off scot-free. Unless you think that society just changed over the course of that two-year period, since then, John Edwards has been roasted for cheating on his wife. Bill Richardson, former governor of the state of New Mexico, who's always mentioned as a possible vice presidential candidate or somebody people consider, never gets picked. You know why? An article pointed it out by one of John Kerry's advisors. He's a womanizer. So let's be clear. Bill from the start has gotten a pass in a way that other people haven't. He's been held to a lower standard, but let's talk about the finances. The Clintons in the 1996 election broke a lot of campaign finance rules, even back before there were campaign financial formats. Media not spending a whole lot of time pointing out Hillary's vociferous statements against gay marriage. Her strong statements against illegal immigration, even though she's flipped. Bill, get this story. This one is the one that kills me. Jeffrey Epstein is a man who is a convicted sex offender, multiple underage women, and he owns a little island that he takes buddies off to and they have orgies, which is legal. They can do what they want. But as part of that, you now have someone who says she was a sex slave who was involved in Bill Clinton was showing up at these parties with underage girls. Is the media covering in a ton? No. Is the Justice Department investigating? No. How about on the finance side, the complete finance side, their Clinton Foundation. There's been reporting by the New York Times. There's been reporting by the Washington Post. There's been reporting by outlets that will endorse Hillary Clinton for president. And they're saying their questions. President Obama's advisers are saying, look, these are legitimate questions. It's a story. Guess what? Justice Department investigating? No, no, they're not. Here's my point. The Clintons, the defense of the Clintons, the argument for the Clintons has always been that sure they have a little bit of corruption, but I like their politics. I want people who believe that. I'm not meaning to trash that opinion. That's an opinion that a lot of Americans have. That's why they have fun feelings about. President Clinton, they like what happened in the 90s and they're willing to overlook some of his personal failings. That's fine. But to claim that the Clintons are held to a higher standard is laughable. With the exception of John F. Kennedy and the Kennedys in general who have gotten away with murder, literally, The Clintons have gotten the freest pass of anyone in American politics since World War II. Not even close, and so to anyone who would say, the Clintons get a pass, they get way too harsh a treatment from the media. I say this.",
