    "turn_text": "you're making our country a worse place. I would expect you not to have a perfect understanding of the First Amendment if you weren't a journalistic institution, somebody who screams First Amendment any time anybody tries to criticize the ridiculous way you characterize a lot of the work that you do. So in light of that, and I'm speaking directly to the Washington Post here, let's give a primer on free speech, the First Amendment and a modern liberal society. First. Freedom of thought and conscience is at the heart of our national identity. The First Amendment pretty much guarantees you the right to think whatever you want and to say whatever you want as long as you're not directly threatening someone else. This means at its core that we are protecting unpopular speech, not popular speech because people aren't going to try to stop popular speech, but they are going to try to stop unpopular speech. This means that our Constitution protects awful people who say offensive things. Flag burning is legal. The Supreme Court's landmark decision on this involves Skokie, Illinois. The Illinois Nazi Party wanted to march in Skokie, Illinois, even though it was a neighborhood that was predominantly Jewish and had Holocaust survivors in it. Think of it. Nazis wanted to march in Skokie, even though it was a town of Holocaust survivors. The Supreme Court said, look, We don't have to agree with this speech, but we'll defend it. The Westboro Baptist who hold signs taunting homosexuals with anti-gay slurs are allowed to do what they do. Is it wrong? Yes, it's morally wrong. They're still allowed to do it. So two fallacies people have about our freedom of speech. The first is people trying to criminalize speech they don't like. We have a couple groups trying to do that right now. For example, in New Hampshire, There is a group trying to criminalize peaceful abortion, anti-abortion protesters, people who stand outside abortion clinics and peacefully protest a group in New Hampshire is trying to get them blocked. The Supreme Court struck that down 9-0, yet the group in New Hampshire is still trying to do it to waste taxpayer funds. We should never criminalize speech just because we don't like it. If it's not directly threatening and directly harmful, and the standard for that is really high, pretty much saying, I'm going to kill you to someone's face, could be considered violation of free speech. Other than that, pretty much allowed to do what you want. That's fallacy number one that we should criminalize. But the second is, we can also be honest about awful people. Let's talk about Charlie Hebdo and this group down in Texas. Both deliberately offend. Both are trying to be offensive. Do they have a right to be protected? Yes. Should the FBI offer them protection? Of course they should. To date the FBI as far as we know, still has not reached out to this group or tried to help them, which is offensive. All citizens are entitled to that protection. That said, there is also nothing wrong. There are groups on the right who are looking at these people saying, oh, they're saints, they're martyrs, they're so nice. They are martyrs for free speech. People who are killed for saying something are martyrs for free speech, but that doesn't mean they're good people. It is okay and even appropriate to stand up for the right to freedom of speech and even acknowledge those who die as martyrs without endorsing what they say. President Obama said this a little while back and it got a lot of conservatives freaking out. Quote, the future does not belong to those who slander the prophet of Islam. Conservatives freak out at this statement because they try to spin it as saying that nobody can criticize Muhammad. That's ridiculous. Conservatives, you're wrong. You're flat wrong here. People who trash other people's beliefs for no other reason other than to trash them and people who spend their lives trying to tear down other people and trying to offend other people end up in the ash bin of history. Look through history. Seriously, take a look through at the people we look at as heroes, and the people we look at as martyrs, and then notice what they don't have in common. For the most part, there are a handful of exceptions I'm meant to quote one of them, but it's the Isaac Newton's. It's the Martin Luther's of the world. It's the Galileo's. People who had a principled objection on one subject and weren't just flagrantly offensive. There's a reason we look back at those people who had principled, thoughtful objections, and were not flagrantly just attacking for the sake of attacking. People who intend to offend and spend their lives doing it, they have a right to do it, they should be protected in doing it. But, and this is the most important point, even though we believe in that free speech, there's nothing wrong with saying they're bad people, for deliberately trying to offend others. I like what this misquote of Voltaire, who's one of the exceptions to the rule I just gave, This is a misquote of him, but it represents the intent of what he often believes. I disapprove of what you say, but I will defend to the death. You're right to say it. That should be the principle that we fight for in this country that people have the right to freedom of speech, they have the right to say what they want, they have the right to believe what they want, they don't have to agree with it, and there's nothing wrong with criticizing. Now last point, need to have a discussion about civil society because independent of the notion of freedom of speech as an element of the First Amendment, which is a matter of law, there's also a cultural question of when should we get up in arms because it's legal to boycott Donald Sterling. You may remember him, he was the owner of the Clippers, a basketball team who said he didn't want black people at his games. Obviously deeply offensive and the NBA forced him out, but this is con to a really unhealthy extreme It's certainly an appropriate reaction in serious cases Imagine somebody who walks in to your business and wants to customer service job public-facing and he has a swastika tattooed on his head I actually met a gentleman who had this I asked him that he said it was the worst decision in my life I can't get a job of course he can't get a job He has a hateful symbol on his forehead, screaming to everybody who comes into the store that they should feel uncomfortable. So, of course, he never gets a job. It could be taken as threatening toward Jewish individuals and minorities. But, as I said a second ago, this has been taken to an unhealthy extreme. For instance, we have a state university, the University of North Texas, students protesting trying to prevent the governor of the state of Texas from speaking at their commencement. Sorry, he's the governor of your state. You're at a state institution. Come on. We also had the Mozilla CEO fired a few years back because he had donated to Prop A $1,000 to the Proposition 8 campaign years and years before that. The intellectual founder of the gay rights movement in America, Andrew Sullivan, wrote, if this is what we've become, I want no part of it after that. People get out of control. So where's the line? Where's the test? Here's the test I'd like to suggest. A little bit of history for you. You may have heard of McCarthyism. Senator Joseph McCarthy from Wisconsin was a senator for 10 years from 1947 to 1957. He held hearings about trying to purge our government of communism. That led to black listing of any communists in Hollywood as well. People who were members of the Communist Party were unable to get jobs. People said, oh, we won't touch a communist. We won't work with them. And society looks at both of these actions and still considers them an abomination. Says it was morally wrong. and McCarthyism has become the name we give to people who go on these witch hunts trying to root out anyone who has an unpopular opinion. So here's my test, and I think in general when we look at these things you should ask, is it worse than communism? And no, I'm not talking about your 2015 communism that your professor says is theoretically peaceful. I'm talking about the communism of the 1950s. Let's be clear what communism has always stood for, but most certainly stood for in the 1950s. Number one, genocide. Conservative estimates put that during the 70 years that communism was really operative on the globe from about 1920 to 1970. Conservative estimates have those communist leaders killing at least 140 million people as part of just oppression, general oppression. 140 million people. That is two and a half holocaust per decade for those 70 years. Number two, there were no freedom of thought or individual rights in these countries. The government could say or do whatever it wanted and do whatever it wanted with you. This included killing and discriminating against homosexuals and other minorities. Finally, communism of that time had an aggressive belief that it should be spread throughout the world through violence. The thought of communism was that there should be violent revolutions in every country to inflict this upon people. So my test for whether you should boycott something, for whether society should tolerate mass boycotting and that is simply this. Was it worse for a person to be a member of the Communist Party or to say or do whatever it is they said or did? I'll let you make your choice on each one of these but I think that's a good moral standard we should all follow. When we come back, Representative Rob Bishop, Congressman from the Great State of Utah, is going to make a little bit of news and talk a little bit about what it's like in Washington. Hey everyone, thanks for downloading the podcast. Do you disagree with something I said? You're probably not alone. Shoot me an email at tpage at politepolitics.com and if you make a good point, I'll read it on the air.",
