  {
    "episode_id": 90235,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90235.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "To be honest, I think Washington is very similar today as it was when I first came back here. I was so naive and a little bit surprised about what I found in Washington. There are two sets of minds, and I think a lot of those work in the bureaucracy, so they stay, the presence come and go that bureaucrats stay. One mindset really wants to try and devolve responsibility and power from the federal level back to the states where I think you have better management decisions being made and people have some kind of access to those decision makers. There is another mindset that simply thinks that everything needs to be local and needs to be consolidated within Washington, but only Washington and obviously because I'm negative here, I don't believe this mindset. but only Washington has the right ideas for the entire country and only Washington is good enough to make those decisions in any conflict between state governments or local governments in Washington, Washington has to win. I reject that, but that is a common mindset. And those mindsets or those two approaches actually stay even as individual players as the president or members of Congress come and go.",
