  {
    "episode_id": 90235,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90235.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "I don't, to be honest with you. I see it more as a statement of degree, I think. If you actually were to talk to the so-called key party people and the establishment people, you'll find like 85 or 95 establishment Republicans were talking about here. I think they would agree on like 85 to 90 percent of the stuff. There may be a difference in tone. There may be a difference in emphasis. I think establishment is the test establishment types are more willing to take small steps to move in the right direction. Sometimes the tea party types want to do it all and want to do it now, and I can understand that mindset as well. So I think it's a matter of degree. I don't really think there's two different camps in the economy. And so I have been called, I've been usually called the tea party type person, but I sometimes have called the staff person because I've been here for a while. And I feel comfortable actually in both modes.",
