  {
    "episode_id": 90235,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90235.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "It's very difficult. And I don't want to sound cheesy, but I do try and pray about it. in making those decisions. So let me give you an example. I'm an old school teacher. No child left behind was passed before I came to Washington and I thought it was a dumb idea when I was teaching and I still think it's a dumb idea. There is an education reform bill going through the House as we speak that does a lot of good things to change, but it's nowhere near what I would like to see as a reform bill. So the question I have to ask myself is, are the reforms that are going in there, good enough to vote for it, or do I hold out for something better, knowing that something better may never actually come along. And so, and that has to be done on a case-by-case basis. There is no matrix that I can use to do with that. Simply, you know, I got feeling of, is are we at least moving in the right direction? It's like Cinder Graham wants told me, and I think you said this before, that if he's trying to go from Washington back home to Texas and he gets as far as Memphis, that's not bad. It's only if he starts from Washington and ends up in Boston, then he's got a problem.",
