  {
    "episode_id": 90235,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90235.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "So when I first got to Washington, I found out the federal government was redoing a military-based runway at Dougway in Utah. But in all the brilliance, we were doing it and from the time the appropriations went to the actual construction, They ran the money, inflation hit. They ran out of money. So they're going to rebuild the runway 2000 feet too short for the landing of the next M16 and emergency landings out there on the success and training branch. They needed that. No one, they all told me, you know, it's too far along the process. You can't get it done. I went back there. I worked with the state of Utah in a way that had never been done before. We tried to break traditions. The end result is I've got the thing built and the extra 2000 feet put in the runway and it has saved lives. So, every once in a while, you got small things like that for which I will always be proud and happy. My overarching goal though has always been I want to reinstate the concepts of federalism so that state and local governments have more control in their decisions and take them out here. And basically, if a person wants to have input into their system, they want to have control of their lives. If the decisions are made in Washington, you don't have control over that. I mean, what can you do? Go back and stand in front of one of the massive apartment buildings in Washington and throw rocks at the windows. If decisions are made at the county level and state level and the city level, then people actually have a chance of having their voices heard and have some kind of say in what goes on in their future. That has always been my goal and I'm going to keep fighting for that goal. And that is a very, it's tough to try and get that done.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
