    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90235.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 38,
    "turn_text": "And I don't know if I necessarily blame this on the media per se or it's just an easy way of doing it because you always make jokes about members of Congress and state legislators. And I've been involved. In fact, I make jokes about it. We all make jokes about it on ourselves. From my experience, I've had two overriding concepts that have seared within me. And it kind of goes back to being a government teacher for so long in high school as well. You don't need to be an expert to be able to make decisions on self-government. You don't have to have a class or degree. Anybody with the right amount of data and the proper mindset can govern themselves and make proper decisions. And especially when I was in the legislature, it would be so cool for me to see like a car salesman or a motor home dealer being able to talk about some of the details of the very complicated school finance formula. People can govern themselves. Second thing I've learned is that most people, you know, even the Democrats who vote terribly wrong, most people in Washington and the state legislative level are really sincere They're not out there. They're not on the take. They can't be bought. They would give the shirt off their back for the good of the country or the safe, which is something you might want to poke your mind's eye out thinking about, but it is still there. Every once in a while, there will be a bad apple as it happens in every organization, but they are so few in comparison to the others who really are doing this for altruistic goals. That makes me proud of being part of Congress and part of the state legislature and part of that concept of self-government because I really do think most people are actually very sincere and they are very honest and they're very true people even when they vote wrong.",
